Nothing was decided this wake. I fixed a broken job chain, launched the remaining page transcriptions, and wrote the scripts the next step needs.

**Check 4 — can a page's slots hold its words if we overfit on 100 pages**
- The overfit training finished all 210 steps. Loss (how far the model is from rebuilding the page text) fell from 0.602 to 0.028, with no errors.
- The loss was still falling (−21% over the last 50 steps; the rule needs within 2%). So the coming recall score (share of the page's words the model can read back from its slots) is only a lower bound. A score of 90% or more would still mean slot capacity is fine. A low score cannot be called a capacity limit.
- The recall job never started. The training job was preempted (stopped by the cluster for another user) and requeued. Its last restart hit the script's "don't overwrite" guard, which marked the whole job failed, so the job waiting on it was stuck. I killed that job and launched the same command again; it is now running.
- I patched the training script: a restart that finds the model already finished now exits cleanly, and each attempt's log is kept. The script was rechecked for syntax but not run yet.

**Check 3 — does adding the page's own text as slots help**
- The first quarter of the page transcriptions is healthy: 317 pages, none empty, 189 words per page on average.
- 28 of 317 pages hit the 1,024-token cap. About 8 of those are the model looping on the same words, and about 12 are dense pages cut short. This will be reported next to the check-3 result.
- The whole check should cost about 6.9 GPU-hours, under the 12 GPU-hour point where I must ask you first. So I launched the other three quarters; they are queued.
- I wrote three new scripts: one builds the three question sets (page images only / page text only / both), and one runs the evaluation of all three on the same machine. They pass syntax checks but have not run yet. The next wake runs a 30-question test of them first.

**Budget:** 19 of 32 jobs and about 10.4 of 30 GPU-hours used. 1.5 of those GPU-hours were lost to the preemption.

**One action was refused:** deleting the 06:00 "usage limit" ALERT file. That alert is out of date (the quota came back and this wake ran), but the file belongs to you, so I left it.

The next wake starts when the recall job and all three transcription jobs have finished (at most 8 hours).

Nothing needed from you. You can delete `campaign_slotcheck/ALERT` whenever you like.
